The Cost Delta in N2 is `=ABS(E2-K2)`, so Time Delta in M2 most likely follows the same pattern, comparing Time A (D2) with Time B (J2).

One caveat: D2 and J2 look like text (e.g. "Jan 5 2012 12:15:32.12"). If Excel doesn't read them as date/times, this formula will return #VALUE!.

{"formula": "=ABS(D2-J2)"}

O11 is headed "Error" with the unit "( OF )", which suggests an objective-function error comparing the calculated pressure (N, kPa) against the experimental pressure (G, kPa). A common choice for that is the squared difference.

P is labelled "ARD (%)", which would then hold the relative deviation, so O13 most likely takes the squared error between the two pressures.

{"formula": "=(N13-G13)^2"}

J5 should hold the cheapest price for the part number in H5. I5 already looks up the person by matching both part number and price against J5, which confirms this.

To get the cheapest price for a part, use MINIFS on the Price column filtered by part number. I'm assuming a version of Excel that supports MINIFS.

{"formula": "=MINIFS($D$3:$D$21,$B$3:$B$21,$H5)"}

Cell FR8 sits in the Area 1 monthly AVG columns. Rows 12 and 13 (Area 2) use the same formula in column FR, so row 8 should follow it with the row reference changed. That formula pulls the start column from FR5 and the end column from FS5−1, uses ROW(FP12) to get the row, and returns blank if the first cell is empty.

For row 8, the matching formula uses ROW(FP8):

{"formula": "=IF(INDIRECT(ADDRESS(ROW(FP8),$FR$5))=\"\",\"\",AVERAGE(INDIRECT(ADDRESS(ROW(FP8),$FR$5) & \":\" & ADDRESS(ROW(FP8),$FS$5-1))))"}

Days of Stock is usually ending inventory divided by average daily demand. Here D8 is the ending inventory and D7 is the monthly demand, so I divide them and multiply by 30 to turn months into days.

I used the current month's demand rather than next month's. Next month's demand would break in December (J9), because there is no K7.

{"formula": "=D8/D7*30"}

U4 sits under the "Average" header, and the rest of row 4 works on the trial values in `$B4:$Q4`, so this formula averages those same cells.

Columns S and T suggest an outlier-filtering step based on the IQR. Their exact use is unclear, so this is a plain average and does not exclude outliers.

{"formula": "=AVERAGE($B4:$Q4)"}

Row 4 is the "Original" row, and H3 is labeled "Marathon Per Km". The adjacent cell I4 computes the marathon pace per 400 m as `=I2/(42195/G4)`, scaling the marathon time in G4 by a distance in row 2.

H4 most likely uses the same pattern with its own distance in H2, presumably 1000 m. That gives the marathon pace per km.

{"formula": "=H2/(42195/G4)"}

The cell to the left (F1) is labelled "Qual Games", so G1 most likely shows how many games are listed. This formula counts the non-blank Bet Team entries in D4:D37.

This is a best guess. Column C marks some rows with "X" instead of a game number. If "X" means a game did not qualify, `=COUNT(C4:C37)` may fit better, since it counts only the numeric game numbers.

{"formula": "=COUNTA(D4:D37)"}

I6 sits next to the label "Number of overall tasks completed" in H6. C4 already counts the "Completed" rows in the Tracks sheet, so the likeliest formula is a direct reference to C4.

The "View statistics between October 9th…" header hints the count might be meant to be date-filtered. However, there's no date column to build that on, so the plain reference is the safest prediction.

{"formula": "=C4"}

B25 most likely counts how many shifts employee A (row 4) starts at the time in B24. The formula looks for cells in B4:CN4 that begin with that time, so "10:00-18:00" counts when B24 is 10:00. B24 is probably a real time value, so `TEXT` turns it into "hh:mm" text before matching.

The mixed references let it fill both ways. Filling right moves to the next start time in row 24. Filling down moves to the next employee's row.

{"formula": "=COUNTIF($B4:$CN4,TEXT(B$24,\"hh:mm\")&\"*\")"}

Column H already uses CUMPRINC for the principal paid in months 1–36, so G3 should be the matching interest figure: CUMIPMT with the same arguments.

The prefix is only `=`, so the formula is completed fresh in the same style as H3. It takes the rate from E3/12, the period count from D5, and the loan amount from F3, covering periods 1 through 36 with type 0.

{"formula": "=CUMIPMT(E3/12,D5,F3,1,36,0)"}

This is a workings block that breaks the row-2 formula into steps. E10 is "Month start date", so F10 should hold the LET's `d` value: the first day of the month named in F9, in the year of $A2.

F9 already extracts the month name with `MID(F1,4,10)`, so the formula can reference F9 directly.

{"formula": "=DATE(YEAR($A2),MONTH(1&F9),1)"}

C7 sits under the "Years of Service" header, and B7 holds the date joined. The formula should count full years from B7 up to the start of the tracking period, which is the date in E6.

Column D's lookup (`{1,10}`) treats anything under 1 as 0 leave days, so a whole-year count is what it expects. DATEDIF with "y" returns completed years. Wrapping it in IFERROR returns 0 when B7 is empty or the employee joined after E6.

{"formula": "=IFERROR(DATEDIF(B7,$E$6,\"y\"),0)"}

Column F is headed "Date Value", so F2 most likely holds just the date part of the "First Join" timestamp in C2. C2 looks like text from an attendance export ("11/20/23, 2:02:13 PM"), so the formula takes everything before the comma and converts it to a date.

This assumes the system reads dates as month/day/year. If C2 is actually a real date-time value rather than text, `=INT(C2)` would be the simpler choice.

{"formula": "=DATEVALUE(LEFT(C2,FIND(\",\",C2)-1))"}

Column C is "Completed Date Serial", so C2 should turn the completed date in B2 into an Excel date serial. The values look like text with a weekday in front, such as "Friday, April 09, 2010 5:34:54 PM". This formula drops everything up to the first comma and converts the rest with `--`. `IFERROR` returns a blank for cells that aren't dates, such as B5 ("No need to take any action") or the empty B14.

The conversion assumes a US date setting, where "April 09, 2010 5:34:54 PM" is recognised as a date.

{"formula": "=IFERROR(--MID(B2,FIND(\",\",B2)+2,LEN(B2)),\"\")"}

G2 is "Days from lease start to today", and J1 holds the date used in the duration calculation. So G3 should count the days from the commencement date in D3 to the date in J1. H3 then runs DATEDIF(0,G3,...) on that day count.

Two candidate start dates exist: the commencement date (D) and the current term start date (B). "Lease start" points to the commencement date, so D3 is the better choice.

{"formula": "=$J$1-D3"}

The end date is the rent start date pushed forward by the tenure in years, so the formula offsets B3 by C3×12 months:

{"formula": "=EDATE(B3,C3*12)"}

If the lease should end the day before the anniversary, subtract one day instead: `=EDATE(B3,C3*12)-1`.

F8 is the first year of IL fees. Rows 24–27 in column F use the pattern `=FV($B$17,COUNT($D$8:$D24)-1, 0, -$B$19*$E24, 0)`, which grows the IL fee in B19 by inflation and prorates it by the year fraction in column E.

Applying that pattern to row 8 gives the formula below. Here `COUNT($D$8:$D8)-1` is 0, so in the first year no inflation is applied.

{"formula": "=FV($B$17,COUNT($D$8:$D8)-1, 0, -$B$19*$E8, 0)"}

The label in I25 reads "CURRENT HCI =", so J25 should hold the current golf handicap index. Under the World Handicap System, that is the average of the 8 lowest of the last 20 score differentials, multiplied by 0.96.

The stroke differentials are in G2:G32. Because there are more than 20 rounds, the formula should look only at the most recent 20. A simple version takes the 8 smallest values from the last 20 rows, G13:G32, using `SMALL` with an array constant. I've left out the 0.96 factor, since current WHS rules no longer apply it and simply average the best 8 of 20.

{"formula": "=AVERAGE(SMALL(G13:G32,{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8}))"}

The CD % in I7 most likely comes from the days-to-CD% table in K2:L5 (headers "Days" in K1 and "Cd %" in L1), looked up with the No Of Days value in H7.

This assumes the Days values in K2:K5 are sorted smallest to largest. LOOKUP needs that order to find the right band. The 0 in L5 fits this: the longest period gets no discount.

{"formula": "=LOOKUP(H7,$K$2:$K$5,$L$2:$L$5)"}

The cell B8 is labelled "Implicit interest rate", so a RATE formula fits best. It uses 36 payments of B3/36 (£2,476.68 each), with the asset cost in C1 as present value and the final payment in B4 as future value.

The schedule actually has 37 rows (11–47), so the final payment may fall one period after the 36th instalment. In that case this rate is a close approximation rather than an exact match.

{"formula": "=RATE(36,-B3/36,C1,-B4)"}

M7 should return the 1st-largest savings for Wisconsin, the site named in J5 ("Wisconsin Top 10"). The formulas in J7:L7 look up rows where the savings equal M7, so M7 has to supply the ranked value itself.

The formula uses an array `LARGE(IF(...))` over G4:G49, filtered to rows where column B matches the first word of J5. The rank comes from `ROWS(M$7:M7)`, so copying it down gives the 2nd, 3rd, and so on through the top 10.

{"formula": "=LARGE(IF($B$4:$B$49=LEFT($J$5,FIND(\" \",$J$5)-1),$G$4:$G$49),ROWS(M$7:M7))"}

I3 sits next to the label "Min (including 0)" in H3, so it should hold the plain minimum of the data in column B (rows 2–366).

The "Start Date" in I2 hints that the range might depend on a date, but no end date is defined anywhere, so the simple whole-range minimum is the most likely intent.

{"formula": "=MIN(B2:B366)"}

The label in L10 asks for the latest patch common to all countries. Since patches are cumulative, a patch is common to all countries only up to the oldest of each country's latest patch date. So the answer is the minimum of the per-country MAXIFS results in M3:M8.

Countries that have no Model A entry return 0 from MAXIFS. The `">0"` condition excludes them so they don't pull the minimum down to 0.

{"formula": "=MINIFS(M3:M8,M3:M8,\">0\")"}

D4 is labelled "Full Months in last year", and B1:B2 holds the last year's start and end dates. So I've taken D6 to be the number of full months Project A (start B6, end C6) falls within the last year.

The formula clips the project to the overlapping period: the later of the two start dates and the earlier of the two end dates. The +1 counts the final day as part of the month. If the project doesn't overlap last year, DATEDIF errors and IFERROR returns 0.

{"formula": "=IFERROR(DATEDIF(MAX($B6,$B$1),MIN($C6,$B$2)+1,\"m\"),0)"}

Row 29 holds the biweekly totals. The neighboring cells follow one pattern: B29 is `=SUM(H16,H27)`, and M29, O29 and Q29 each sum their column's row 16 and row 27 values.

G29 sits under the "Biweekly Total Hrs." label, so it should add the two weekly totals in column G (G16 and G27).

{"formula": "=SUM(G16,G27)"}

The header in E1 asks for turnaround time excluding weekends. The most natural measure is the working days from Order Date (B2) to Completion Date (D2), which `NETWORKDAYS` gives directly.

The vacation dates in column F are free-text ranges like "December 12 to December 22", so they can't be passed as a holidays argument. I've left them out.

{"formula": "=NETWORKDAYS(B2,D2)"}

Row 204 points back to `$D$72` and labels it "NPV", and the rows below search for where that value changes sign to find the cost of energy. So D72 is probably the NPV of the after-tax equity cash flows in row 67.

I'm assuming the discount rate is `Inputs!$G$62`, because B72 pulls from that cell, but the sheet doesn't confirm that cell holds the rate. `F67` is the year-0 equity investment, so it is added outside `NPV`, which would otherwise discount it a year.

{"formula": "=NPV(Inputs!$G$62,G67:AJ67)+F67"}

Time-weighted return is found by linking each period's return together. That means multiplying (1 + monthly TWR) for Nov, Dec and Jan (F6:F8), then subtracting 1.

I didn't use `E8/E5-1` because the TWR Bal values in column E are not chained across periods (for example, `E7=C7-B7`). That ratio would not give the compounded return.

{"formula": "=(1+F6)*(1+F7)*(1+F8)-1"}

The headers suggest a Hohmann transfer between radii Ra (K3) and Rb (J3). L3 already gives the first burn, at Ra. M3 ("DelV b") should be the second burn, made when the craft reaches Rb.

The second burn is √(μ/Rb)·(1 − √(2·Ra/(Ra+Rb))). The formula takes μ from the same cell L3 uses ('Propulsion Subsystem Sizing'!$J$3), with Rb = J3 and Ra = K3.

{"formula": "=SQRT('Propulsion Subsystem Sizing'!$J$3/J3)*(1-SQRT(2*K3/(J3+K3)))"}

I13 sits under the "YTD Interest" header, so it should hold a running total of interest that restarts each calendar year. Interest is in column E and the year is in column H.

Using SUMIFS with the year criterion and a range anchored at row 13 gives a year-to-date total that stays correct when the formula is copied down the column.

{"formula": "=SUMIFS($E$13:E13,$H$13:H13,H13)"}

Cell P3 sits under the QUANTITY header, with the selected fruit in O3 and a date range in P1:Q1. The country is selected in U1, the cell next to the COUNTRY label in T1.

Quantities live in D2:M29, with dates across D1:M1, countries in B2:B29 and products in C2:C29. So the formula should sum the rows matching the fruit and country, restricted to columns whose date falls between P1 and Q1. SUMPRODUCT handles this:

{"formula": "=SUMPRODUCT(($C$2:$C$29=O3)*($B$2:$B$29=$U$1)*($D$1:$M$1>=$P$1)*($D$1:$M$1<=$Q$1)*$D$2:$M$29)"}

H6 sits under the "Trim Mean 10%" header, and G6 holds the task name, so this should be a 10% trimmed mean of Time (C2:C61) for rows where Task (B2:B61) matches G6. Absolute references let the formula copy down to G7 and G8.

{"formula": "=TRIMMEAN(IF($B$2:$B$61=G6,$C$2:$C$61),10%)"}

In older Excel versions this needs to be entered as an array formula (Ctrl+Shift+Enter).

The best guess is a lookup of the row's phase (G2) in the phase table at A8:B11, which returns that phase's % of Business. This is the same formula as column I, which is labelled "old (delete column)". H2 is probably meant to replace it.

This is uncertain. The "Checking" column compares H to L, and L holds stepped values that rise gradually between phases. A plain lookup won't match those values in every row, so H2 may need an interpolation formula instead.

{"formula": "=VLOOKUP(G2,$A$8:$B$11,2,FALSE)"}

H9 sits in the "n" column of the distribution table for variable "a", so it should count how many values in column B fall in the range F9 to G9. The first range runs from the minimum to minimum+1, and the next range starts at G9, so a half-open interval avoids counting boundary values twice: include F9, exclude G9.

The last range ends at MAX(B:B), so it would need `<=` instead, but this cell is the first row, so `>=F9` and `<G9` is right.

{"formula": "=COUNTIFS($B$4:$B$63,\">=\"&F9,$B$4:$B$63,\"<\"&G9)"}

